00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:20.340
You don't have to just be Godzilla, and then you are, like, set up in a particular area of Japan or other famous Godzilla landmarks, and everything in the area is destructible, but also, like, you totally have unique moves to beat the piss out of the other kaiju monsters.

00:47:20.340 --> 00:47:36.220
So that is, like, a very rewarding, cathartic Godzilla experience where you can actually take out your frustrations on the fellow kaiju monsters, where in this game I feel like I'm just hitting buttons and the enemies are glowing red to imply that something has happened to them.

00:47:38.620 --> 00:47:44.280
But while stumbling for Godzilla games, because there's so many of them, and I was like, hmm, which ones do you handpick?

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:51.920
I found out about this game called Kyohei Toshi, a game that I can't believe exists.

00:47:51.960 --> 00:47:53.980
It never came to North America.

00:47:53.980 --> 00:48:03.760
It will probably never be translated because it has so much dialogue, sort of, you know, takes a really passionate person to translate a game like this, and I doubt it would happen.

00:48:03.940 --> 00:48:05.340
But just watch a video.

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:07.060
I included a video on the show notes here.

00:48:07.460 --> 00:48:10.460
This is like Cloverfield, the video game.

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:14.480
The objective of the game is to escape a city.

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:29.880
You're just a regular human being, and all you have to do is try to escape a city ravaged by battles between giant kaiju, giant robots, and heroes from other famous live action and animated Japanese film and TV series.

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:38.680
So you have the Godzilla series, the Ultra series, Gamera, Neon Genesis Evangelion, or however you say that.

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:46.640
You have all of these series coming together as if, like, all of them just came to our Earth all at once.

00:48:46.660 --> 00:48:54.140
But what I really appreciate about it is it's like, imagine Yakuza, like, that kind of scale of, like, what Tokyo looks like and everything.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:49:04.520
But then the actual kaiju or Ultraman, for instance, like, are actually giant and massive, and you just can't comprehend the scale.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:10.440
I think it really puts in perspective just how fucked we'd all be if this happened in real life.

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:17.480
Because, yeah, while the game doesn't really damage the main player, it causes a lot of destruction to Tokyo in a meaningful way.

00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:19.540
So I was really surprised by this game.

00:49:19.540 --> 00:49:23.940
And Sean and Joe, I know I just sent you guys a video, so you haven't had too much time to digest.

